So, welcome, thank you.

00:01:01.211 --> 00:01:03.485
&gt;&gt; Yes. Thank you very much Josh.

00:01:03.486 --> 00:01:05.500
Thank you for having me here.

00:01:05.501 --> 00:01:07.805
So, I will talk about

00:01:07.806 --> 00:01:09.755
Multi-party Computation
Research at

00:01:09.756 --> 00:01:11.995
the Technical University
of Eindhoven,

00:01:11.996 --> 00:01:14.315
and we are there
in the group with

00:01:14.316 --> 00:01:17.820
Berry Schoenmakers from Rome
and Universiteit Leiden.

00:01:17.821 --> 00:01:20.600
So, many of the things
that I will talk

00:01:20.601 --> 00:01:24.205
about is joint work with them.

00:01:24.206 --> 00:01:27.770
The plan for today is to present

00:01:27.771 --> 00:01:30.649
you MPyC which is
a Python framework

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:33.660
developed mainly by
Berry Schoenmakers

00:01:33.661 --> 00:01:37.560
for performing
Multi-Party Computation,

00:01:37.561 --> 00:01:39.835
and this framework is not so much

00:01:39.836 --> 00:01:43.400
tailored to a
production environment,

00:01:43.401 --> 00:01:45.980
but more as a tool for

00:01:45.981 --> 00:01:49.340
research in a rapid
prototyping environments.

00:01:49.341 --> 00:01:52.190
Then I will talk about performing

00:01:52.191 --> 00:01:56.415
linear algebra over the
rationals in an MPC setting,

00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:58.620
and if time allows,

00:01:58.621 --> 00:01:59.840
I will say something about

00:01:59.841 --> 00:02:02.730
a comparison with
secure comparison,

00:02:02.731 --> 00:02:05.850
but if there is no time and
you're interested in that,

00:02:05.851 --> 00:02:08.425
so, please see me after the talk.

00:02:08.426 --> 00:02:13.230
So, let's motivate MPyC or MPC,

00:02:13.231 --> 00:02:17.040
why do we want to have
Multi-Party Computation?

00:02:17.041 --> 00:02:19.270
At the following example,

00:02:19.271 --> 00:02:22.820
let us suppose that
the US Census Bureau wants

00:02:22.821 --> 00:02:27.124
to compute statistics
over medical records.

00:02:27.125 --> 00:02:30.950
Well, in a world without MPC,

00:02:30.951 --> 00:02:33.335
the hospitals will have to send

00:02:33.336 --> 00:02:36.200
the medical records to
the Census Bureau or

00:02:36.201 --> 00:02:39.140
the Census Bureau
Query Stem and then

00:02:39.141 --> 00:02:42.610
compute the statistics
that it wants to compute.

00:02:42.611 --> 00:02:45.580
But with Multi-Party Computation,

00:02:45.581 --> 00:02:51.529
we can have a better situation
in terms of data privacy,

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:54.380
namely the hospitals can run

00:02:54.381 --> 00:02:58.180
a interactive protocol together
with the Census Bureau,

00:02:58.181 --> 00:03:01.100
so that the Census Bureau

00:03:01.101 --> 00:03:03.425
gets in the end to
the desired statistics.

00:03:03.426 --> 00:03:06.035
By the way, the hospitals
and the Census Bureau

00:03:06.036 --> 00:03:09.970
agree on what statistics are
allowed to be computable,

00:03:09.971 --> 00:03:12.980
however, the Census Bureau

00:03:12.981 --> 00:03:17.460
does nothing beyond
this statistic.

00:03:17.461 --> 00:03:20.120
So, let's describe this a bit

00:03:20.121 --> 00:03:22.920
more formally or maybe
forgets the application,

00:03:22.921 --> 00:03:25.950
let's just talk about
what MPC can do.

00:03:25.951 --> 00:03:29.130
So, here we talk about

00:03:29.131 --> 00:03:32.905
three parties that each
holds a private input,

00:03:32.906 --> 00:03:35.435
and the parties want to compute

00:03:35.436 --> 00:03:39.230
some function that takes
these private inputs

00:03:39.231 --> 00:03:43.100
such that no party learns
more than what can

00:03:43.101 --> 00:03:47.175
be deduced from her input and
the output of the function.

00:03:47.176 --> 00:03:50.420
So, there's an
example we could be

00:03:50.421 --> 00:03:53.335
interested in learning
the sum of those inputs,

00:03:53.336 --> 00:03:57.470
or the sum and some
product as given

00:03:57.471 --> 00:03:59.510
there by F2 or

00:03:59.511 --> 00:04:02.110
maybe even what you want
to make a comparison,

00:04:02.111 --> 00:04:06.060
but let's not talk about
that yet in this talk.

00:04:06.061 --> 00:04:10.035
Yeah. Well, how to
how to achieve this.

00:04:10.036 --> 00:04:15.160
So, Multi-party Computation
comes in two flavors;

00:04:15.161 --> 00:04:18.535
Garbled circuits and
Arithmetic Secret Sharing.

00:04:18.536 --> 00:04:21.394
I don't want to
exhaustively explain

00:04:21.395 --> 00:04:25.340
what especially the
first type means,

00:04:25.341 --> 00:04:29.780
but I just want to say to
the experts in this field,

00:04:29.781 --> 00:04:31.850
I want to scope the talk
that I only want

00:04:31.851 --> 00:04:35.360
to talk about Arithmetic
Secret Sharing here.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:41.010
Well, Arithmetic Secret Sharing
goes back actually

00:04:41.011 --> 00:04:43.310
to a paper by Blakley and also

00:04:43.311 --> 00:04:46.800
a paper by Shamir
from the late '70s.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.570
Here the question
is, how can we share

00:04:50.571 --> 00:04:52.810
a secret among several players?

00:04:52.811 --> 00:04:54.110
Well, we can do that with

00:04:54.111 --> 00:04:56.580
the polynomials in
the following way,

00:04:56.581 --> 00:05:00.170
we assume that we work
over a finite field,

00:05:00.171 --> 00:05:03.460
here I took the integers
modulo prime,

00:05:03.461 --> 00:05:08.660
and I choose the prime

00:05:08.661 --> 00:05:11.775
strictly larger than
the number of players n,

00:05:11.776 --> 00:05:14.780
and I choose
a threshold parameter,

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:16.655
which is an integer,

00:05:16.656 --> 00:05:19.615
which is strictly
smaller than n over two,

00:05:19.616 --> 00:05:23.330
and we are here targeting
the passively secure case,

00:05:23.331 --> 00:05:27.255
which means that the parties
they follow the protocol,

00:05:27.256 --> 00:05:29.600
but still, they tried to learn

00:05:29.601 --> 00:05:33.195
as much as possible from
all the protocol transcripts.

00:05:33.196 --> 00:05:38.350
Now, we choose a random
polynomial up to degree t,

00:05:38.351 --> 00:05:41.330
such that the polynomial

00:05:41.331 --> 00:05:44.735
evaluated at the point
zero equals the secret.

00:05:44.736 --> 00:05:47.690
As you can think of a picture in

00:05:47.691 --> 00:05:50.765
your head which
doesn't make sense

00:05:50.766 --> 00:05:53.150
completely because if we
are here working over

00:05:53.151 --> 00:05:57.425
finite fields so the dashed
part doesn't exist of course.

00:05:57.426 --> 00:06:01.475
But in this way, we could
evaluate the polynomial

00:06:01.476 --> 00:06:06.025
in other points than zero
and give the evaluations.

00:06:06.026 --> 00:06:10.170
So, the y value to the
different parties,

00:06:10.171 --> 00:06:12.470
and if we evaluate
that polynomial

00:06:12.471 --> 00:06:15.570
at enough places namely;

00:06:15.571 --> 00:06:17.985
t plus one points,

00:06:17.986 --> 00:06:22.545
then those evaluations
define the polynomial and

00:06:22.546 --> 00:06:27.270
allows the parties to reconstruct

00:06:27.271 --> 00:06:29.765
the polynomial and
reconstruct with that

00:06:29.766 --> 00:06:33.405
the secret by evaluating
the polynomial at point zero.

00:06:33.406 --> 00:06:36.105
On the other hand, t
evaluation points,

00:06:36.106 --> 00:06:39.020
they don't give any
information about the secret,

00:06:39.021 --> 00:06:41.160
so, this is the
threshold property.

00:06:41.161 --> 00:06:41.460
&gt;&gt; Can I ask quickly why you

00:06:41.461 --> 00:06:44.460
are restricting the thresholds to

00:06:44.461 --> 00:06:46.460
less than half the
parameters that's

00:06:46.461 --> 00:06:48.460
nearly larger to
less than n of two?

00:06:48.461 --> 00:06:53.260
&gt;&gt; Yes, I wanted

00:06:53.261 --> 00:06:57.110
to make the parameters
concrete here.

00:06:57.111 --> 00:06:57.575
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:06:57.576 --> 00:07:02.250
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, a bit of notation.

00:07:02.251 --> 00:07:05.330
So, I use this double brackets

00:07:05.331 --> 00:07:07.680
for a secret shared value so,

00:07:07.681 --> 00:07:10.480
this means that a is
from the finite field,

00:07:10.481 --> 00:07:14.500
and the parties each
hold a share of a.

00:07:14.501 --> 00:07:16.030
Then I can perform

00:07:16.031 --> 00:07:20.335
operations with
those secret shared values,

00:07:20.336 --> 00:07:21.905
I can perform addition

00:07:21.906 --> 00:07:24.020
which is actually
local computation, so,

00:07:24.021 --> 00:07:26.030
there is no interaction required

00:07:26.031 --> 00:07:28.585
between the players to
compute the addition,

00:07:28.586 --> 00:07:33.590
and there's multiplication
which is more expensive in

00:07:33.591 --> 00:07:36.200
the sense that now
the parties have

00:07:36.201 --> 00:07:39.070
to communicate to each other.

00:07:39.071 --> 00:07:41.930
The reason for this
is that if you do

00:07:41.931 --> 00:07:45.585
point-wise multiplication
of a polynomial,

00:07:45.586 --> 00:07:47.975
then you end up with
a higher degree.

00:07:47.976 --> 00:07:50.285
So, you need some protocol to

00:07:50.286 --> 00:07:53.205
bring back the degree to
what you started with,

00:07:53.206 --> 00:07:56.685
and that requires
the communication.

00:07:56.686 --> 00:07:59.330
So, if you want to perform

00:07:59.331 --> 00:08:02.330
computations with
MPC and practice,

00:08:02.331 --> 00:08:04.335
then it's convenient
to have some kind of

00:08:04.336 --> 00:08:07.115
framework that helps you with

00:08:07.116 --> 00:08:09.590
implementing all this
communication logic

00:08:09.591 --> 00:08:12.290
and arithmetic over
finite fields.

00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:16.355
So, hence there are
MPC frameworks,

00:08:16.356 --> 00:08:20.000
and here is a list
of such frameworks.

00:08:20.001 --> 00:08:23.855
The first one was Fairplay from

00:08:23.856 --> 00:08:27.710
2004 and since then there
have been several frameworks,

00:08:27.711 --> 00:08:29.885
and these frameworks are

00:08:29.886 --> 00:08:31.960
all for Arithmetic
Secret Sharing.

00:08:31.961 --> 00:08:37.020
So, there are also Garbled
circuits based MPC frameworks,

00:08:37.021 --> 00:08:39.915
but I don't mention them here.

00:08:39.916 --> 00:08:44.390
In this talk, I want to
present to you MPyC which

00:08:44.391 --> 00:08:46.210
is mainly developed by

00:08:46.211 --> 00:08:49.459
Berry Schoenmakers
from the TU Eindhoven,

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:52.010
and was inspired by VIFF,

00:08:52.011 --> 00:08:54.290
which is also mentioned here in

00:08:54.291 --> 00:08:58.100
the list which was

00:08:58.101 --> 00:09:02.820
from Eindhoven University
from Geisler et al.

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:06.125
So, if we classify

00:09:06.126 --> 00:09:10.215
MPC frameworks within
The Arithmetic Secret Sharing,

00:09:10.216 --> 00:09:12.320
then we could distinguish between

00:09:12.321 --> 00:09:14.990
Library-based
frameworks and Domain

00:09:14.991 --> 00:09:18.065
specific language
based frameworks.

00:09:18.066 --> 00:09:21.294
So, in a Library-based framework,

00:09:21.295 --> 00:09:24.875
the MPyC functions are
just library calls,

00:09:24.876 --> 00:09:27.470
and you can mix those calls

00:09:27.471 --> 00:09:31.740
with features that
the host language offers you.

00:09:31.741 --> 00:09:34.020
So, for example, if you need to

00:09:34.021 --> 00:09:36.800
compute a genre symbol then you

00:09:36.801 --> 00:09:41.840
just use the feature provided
by some other library,

00:09:41.841 --> 00:09:45.015
and you can mix that with
your MPyC protocols.

00:09:45.016 --> 00:09:48.120
Whereas, there are also
frameworks out there who

00:09:48.121 --> 00:09:51.695
offer a Domain specific language,

00:09:51.696 --> 00:09:54.940
and the latter might be better

00:09:54.941 --> 00:09:58.150
suited for correctness
verification.

00:09:58.151 --> 00:10:00.390
But on the other end,
if you need something

00:10:00.391 --> 00:10:02.410
that is already there
in the host language,

00:10:02.411 --> 00:10:04.745
then you first need some way to

00:10:04.746 --> 00:10:07.660
import that or make
it available in

00:10:07.661 --> 00:10:14.630
your little world with
the specific language.

00:10:14.631 --> 00:10:18.725
But yes, both approaches
have their pros and cons,

00:10:18.726 --> 00:10:23.630
but MPyC is a framework
of the first kind.

00:10:23.631 --> 00:10:29.015
So, let's talk about how
MPyC works under the hood,

00:10:29.016 --> 00:10:31.535
about the Execution Model.

00:10:31.536 --> 00:10:36.000
So, if I want to compute
say two products

00:10:36.001 --> 00:10:37.340
in succession right here

00:10:37.341 --> 00:10:40.130
the E depends on
the value of C. So,

00:10:40.131 --> 00:10:41.700
first I have to compute C,

00:10:41.701 --> 00:10:44.595
and then I can compute E.

00:10:44.596 --> 00:10:46.480
If I will do that in

00:10:46.481 --> 00:10:50.095
a synchronous
programming environment,

00:10:50.096 --> 00:10:54.040
then you basically have to wait

00:10:54.041 --> 00:10:57.500
till C is available
and only then you can

00:10:57.501 --> 00:11:00.990
start computing E. Because here

00:11:00.991 --> 00:11:02.630
a multiplication

00:11:02.631 --> 00:11:07.050
involves communication
between the parties,

00:11:07.051 --> 00:11:09.920
you have to actually wait for

00:11:09.921 --> 00:11:11.450
this packets to go out to

00:11:11.451 --> 00:11:13.800
the other parties and
the other parties do something,

00:11:13.801 --> 00:11:15.325
and then send you
the packet back,

00:11:15.326 --> 00:11:18.790
before you can start
your next multiplication.

00:11:18.791 --> 00:11:21.360
So, this would be slow,

00:11:21.361 --> 00:11:25.650
and hence VIFF
introduced this and

00:11:25.651 --> 00:11:30.045
also MPyC uses
the asynchronous model,

00:11:30.046 --> 00:11:32.765
where you create a task

00:11:32.766 --> 00:11:37.930
for an operation
that would require

00:11:37.931 --> 00:11:42.460
network communication and
it would immediately return

00:11:42.461 --> 00:11:44.880
you some objects that

00:11:44.881 --> 00:11:47.775
indicates that you will get
that value in the future.

00:11:47.776 --> 00:11:49.955
In Python that is
called the Future,

00:11:49.956 --> 00:11:52.735
or in some other languages
it's called an Awaitable,

00:11:52.736 --> 00:11:55.630
but it's all part
of the same idea.

00:11:55.631 --> 00:11:57.675
So, you can build up

00:11:57.676 --> 00:12:00.570
a computation graph
with these futures,

00:12:00.571 --> 00:12:07.050
and then at the end await
the future and get the results.

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:11.070
There's also a notion
of coroutines that you

00:12:11.071 --> 00:12:15.760
can map these kind of
operations on a single thread,

00:12:15.761 --> 00:12:18.860
if you want or on a thread pool.

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:25.205
But the benefits of this
asynchronous execution model,

00:12:25.206 --> 00:12:26.950
is that you can already look

00:12:26.951 --> 00:12:28.870
ahead and see what is coming,

00:12:28.871 --> 00:12:30.490
and you can already

00:12:30.491 --> 00:12:34.510
execute local work which you
might need in the future.

00:12:34.511 --> 00:12:38.950
So, for example, if your
protocol requires a random bits,

00:12:38.951 --> 00:12:43.815
you might do some or you might
require a pre-computation,

00:12:43.816 --> 00:12:46.500
that you can already carry out.

00:12:46.501 --> 00:12:50.140
So, it's in some sense
a hybrid form

00:12:50.141 --> 00:12:52.275
of greedy and lazy execution.

00:12:52.276 --> 00:12:53.780
You want to be greedy in

00:12:53.781 --> 00:12:55.490
the sense that all
the local work that you have

00:12:55.491 --> 00:12:59.585
to perform before sending
out a network packet,

00:12:59.586 --> 00:13:01.470
you want to do greedily,

00:13:01.471 --> 00:13:04.655
while all the work
that would come after

00:13:04.656 --> 00:13:08.400
the response of
your fellow parties,

00:13:08.401 --> 00:13:11.380
that you schedule lazily.

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:18.595
So, you can push all kinds
of work on the CPU,

00:13:18.596 --> 00:13:21.565
or you can make or
keep the CPU busy,

00:13:21.566 --> 00:13:23.000
even though you are waiting

00:13:23.001 --> 00:13:26.380
for network packets to arrive.

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:29.120
Oh there's a question.
Yes, please.

00:13:29.121 --> 00:13:32.770
&gt;&gt; Do you also combine jobs
the network communications

00:13:32.771 --> 00:13:35.170
so that you have
fewer connections

00:13:35.171 --> 00:13:36.895
sending more data at once?

00:13:36.896 --> 00:13:38.800
&gt;&gt; There's a question, whether

00:13:38.801 --> 00:13:40.945
we amortize network traffic?

00:13:40.946 --> 00:13:44.680
So, whether we combine
several things in a packet.

00:13:44.681 --> 00:13:49.145
Yes, that's also a method
that you can use,

00:13:49.146 --> 00:13:55.040
and I know that VIFF uses that
for some operations but I,

00:13:55.041 --> 00:13:57.210
all right sorry,
MPyC uses that for

00:13:57.211 --> 00:14:00.860
some operations maybe
not for all but that

00:14:00.861 --> 00:14:03.820
is indeed a technique that
you can use as well to

00:14:03.821 --> 00:14:06.830
make things more efficient.

00:14:06.831 --> 00:14:08.560
But on the other hand,
you could also say,

00:14:08.561 --> 00:14:12.720
that a network packet is
quite small in itself.

00:14:12.721 --> 00:14:15.300
So, also the operating system,

00:14:15.301 --> 00:14:18.175
does some chunking there,

00:14:18.176 --> 00:14:25.440
it cuts the stream in packets.

00:14:25.441 --> 00:14:28.965
So, as long as you make sure
that you fill that pipe,

00:14:28.966 --> 00:14:32.500
that stream, then you get

00:14:32.501 --> 00:14:36.565
that behavior that you
suggest, all of it.

00:14:36.566 --> 00:14:37.510
&gt;&gt; Because you have

00:14:37.511 --> 00:14:40.600
long-lived connection
between adhesive

00:14:40.601 --> 00:14:42.820
&gt;&gt; Yes, there is
a long-lived connection

00:14:42.821 --> 00:14:44.240
between all the parties.

00:14:44.241 --> 00:14:50.400
Yes. So, this asynchronous model

00:14:50.401 --> 00:14:55.530
has these benefits so that
you can keep the CPUs busy.

00:14:55.531 --> 00:15:01.440
However, you need some way
of throttle the computation.

00:15:01.441 --> 00:15:04.120
In the sense that if
you would set out

00:15:04.121 --> 00:15:10.415
all the work that your algorithm
is going to require

00:15:10.416 --> 00:15:14.470
at once then you might
fill up the memory if

00:15:14.471 --> 00:15:20.230
this executor goes greedily
through your code.

00:15:20.231 --> 00:15:24.910
Say through a long loop and
keeps setting out new work,

00:15:24.911 --> 00:15:26.950
and then at some point
you fill up

00:15:26.951 --> 00:15:29.485
the memory so you need
to throttle there.

00:15:29.486 --> 00:15:32.270
But there's also
a mechanism in MPyC,

00:15:32.271 --> 00:15:36.440
called Barrier, which is
meant for that purpose.

00:15:36.441 --> 00:15:42.470
Okay, these we're some slides
about the theory of MPyC.

00:15:42.471 --> 00:15:46.440
Let's see it in practice.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:54.530
For that I'm going to
switch to a different view.

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:11.200
So, here I am in a
Jupyter notebook

00:16:11.201 --> 00:16:16.210
and one of the nice
features of MPyC,

00:16:16.211 --> 00:16:22.055
is that it can run in
a single party mode in Jupyter.

00:16:22.056 --> 00:16:25.450
This is maybe a bit
counter-intuitive because we do

00:16:25.451 --> 00:16:28.420
Multi-Party Computation then
why would you be interested

00:16:28.421 --> 00:16:31.975
in running a single party mode.

00:16:31.976 --> 00:16:36.290
Well, that is very useful
for debugging purposes.

00:16:36.291 --> 00:16:38.165
So, it has the same API.

00:16:38.166 --> 00:16:40.170
So, exactly the
same script can be

00:16:40.171 --> 00:16:43.860
run in multiple
instances of Python,

00:16:43.861 --> 00:16:45.260
and of course you need

00:16:45.261 --> 00:16:48.060
some small configuration
file to tell at

00:16:48.061 --> 00:16:50.570
which ports those different notes

00:16:50.571 --> 00:16:52.910
are listening so that
they can find each other,

00:16:52.911 --> 00:16:54.940
but when you set
that up you can run

00:16:54.941 --> 00:16:59.500
exactly the same code as in
this single-player mode.

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:03.655
By the way, if you
are in a notebook and

00:17:03.656 --> 00:17:06.260
you haven't installed
it you can install

00:17:06.261 --> 00:17:14.965
it by this single line of
code, this!pip install MPyC.

00:17:14.966 --> 00:17:18.880
So here, I'm going to
import the package,

00:17:18.881 --> 00:17:22.435
I'm going to define
a secret integer type,

00:17:22.436 --> 00:17:25.040
and now I can perform
a computation.

00:17:25.041 --> 00:17:27.400
So, I can make a value three,

00:17:27.401 --> 00:17:28.724
make a value five,

00:17:28.725 --> 00:17:32.180
which are secret integers
and compute the product,

00:17:32.181 --> 00:17:34.680
and this MPyC output will say

00:17:34.681 --> 00:17:37.070
that it reveals the product.

00:17:37.071 --> 00:17:39.820
So, I open the result
immediately.

00:17:39.821 --> 00:17:42.245
Okay, nothing happens so far.

00:17:42.246 --> 00:17:45.000
I created the Z or Z,

00:17:45.001 --> 00:17:51.410
I can inspect what to Z means.

00:17:51.411 --> 00:17:53.550
It's a co-routine object.

00:17:53.551 --> 00:17:57.350
So, that means that
here we were greedily,

00:17:57.351 --> 00:17:59.520
so we we've returned immediately
but nothing happened,

00:17:59.521 --> 00:18:02.360
I still have to run
that co-routine.

00:18:02.361 --> 00:18:05.220
So, let us do that
with this line,

00:18:05.221 --> 00:18:08.135
and I get the value 15.

00:18:08.136 --> 00:18:11.590
Okay, now you might wonder
what is going on here

00:18:11.591 --> 00:18:13.155
because well you have

00:18:13.156 --> 00:18:15.390
multiplied the numbers
three and five,

00:18:15.391 --> 00:18:17.710
and it's 15, I believe that.

00:18:17.711 --> 00:18:20.195
But where is the randomness here?

00:18:20.196 --> 00:18:23.465
Because here there's
only a single party

00:18:23.466 --> 00:18:25.465
who inputs three and five,

00:18:25.466 --> 00:18:27.000
and if you would now copy

00:18:27.001 --> 00:18:30.325
this instance to
say three parties,

00:18:30.326 --> 00:18:32.530
it would still be three and five.

00:18:32.531 --> 00:18:34.130
So, the parties would

00:18:34.131 --> 00:18:37.150
actually share
a constant polynomial,

00:18:37.151 --> 00:18:39.455
so that's not really useful.

00:18:39.456 --> 00:18:43.380
Well, it is useful for
development purposes again,

00:18:43.381 --> 00:18:46.880
but we keep in mind that
this is a simplification,

00:18:46.881 --> 00:18:49.260
because if you would really

00:18:49.261 --> 00:18:52.835
use this in a multiparty setting,

00:18:52.836 --> 00:18:56.170
you would have to
branch on the party ID,

00:18:56.171 --> 00:18:58.290
and the parties will of course

00:18:58.291 --> 00:19:01.310
input different data because
that's the entire purpose

00:19:01.311 --> 00:19:03.380
of doing Multi-Party
Computation that

00:19:03.381 --> 00:19:07.135
different parties have
different pieces of information.

00:19:07.136 --> 00:19:12.135
But again, this is
just a nice simplification,

00:19:12.136 --> 00:19:14.900
that we can use to
input values and then

00:19:14.901 --> 00:19:17.660
we can compute with
those values as

00:19:17.661 --> 00:19:20.570
if we were computing
with values that

00:19:20.571 --> 00:19:25.030
are originating originating
from a single-player.

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:29.375
Well, another example
here is that I can

00:19:29.376 --> 00:19:34.680
create a secret
random field element,

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:40.250
and this happens to be
the random field element.

00:19:40.251 --> 00:19:45.640
By the way, I can inspect
in what fields I'm working.

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:49.715
&gt;&gt; So, that X is secret shared?

00:19:49.716 --> 00:19:52.125
&gt;&gt; X is secret shared, indeed.

00:19:52.126 --> 00:20:02.950
Yes. Here we see that we
have a 64 bit modulus,

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:07.230
and I can inspect more,

00:20:07.231 --> 00:20:10.830
I can inspect the bit length
of the secret integer,

00:20:10.831 --> 00:20:12.850
which is only 32 bits.

00:20:12.851 --> 00:20:15.965
That again, seems to
contradict each other,

00:20:15.966 --> 00:20:19.235
but the reason why
this bit length

00:20:19.236 --> 00:20:23.570
which conveys to usable bit
length for the user,

00:20:23.571 --> 00:20:25.115
is only 32 bits,

00:20:25.116 --> 00:20:27.430
because we have
this headroom that you need

00:20:27.431 --> 00:20:30.530
for a secure comparison operator.

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:33.540
Well, I've got another example

00:20:33.541 --> 00:20:37.530
here to create a random bit.

00:20:37.531 --> 00:20:42.485
Well, it seems to
be a random bit.

00:20:42.486 --> 00:20:45.670
Also here, I show
an implementation of

00:20:45.671 --> 00:20:48.625
a protocol that could
create a random bit.

00:20:48.626 --> 00:20:50.960
So, here I start with creating

00:20:50.961 --> 00:20:54.445
a secret shared field element.

00:20:54.446 --> 00:20:58.535
Then I open the product

00:20:58.536 --> 00:21:01.740
or the square of
that field element.

00:21:01.741 --> 00:21:04.855
Then I compute the inverse

00:21:04.856 --> 00:21:08.265
of the square root
of that square.

00:21:08.266 --> 00:21:12.950
Then I multiply X,

00:21:12.951 --> 00:21:15.345
the original random field element

00:21:15.346 --> 00:21:19.405
with this inverted square root,

00:21:19.406 --> 00:21:22.870
which gives me minus
one or plus one at random.

00:21:22.871 --> 00:21:24.670
Then with this small
transformation,

00:21:24.671 --> 00:21:26.100
plus one divided by two,

00:21:26.101 --> 00:21:29.590
I go to two bits.

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:41.270
I even have here
a small example of comparison.

00:21:41.271 --> 00:21:43.555
So, I have two values,
one and two,

00:21:43.556 --> 00:21:45.930
and I want to compare them

00:21:45.931 --> 00:21:48.885
together or compare
them to each other.

00:21:48.886 --> 00:21:55.690
Indeed, X is not
strictly bigger than Y,

00:21:55.691 --> 00:21:57.370
so I get a zero here.

00:21:57.371 --> 00:22:00.150
If I would change the code,

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:03.235
then I get a one.

00:22:03.236 --> 00:22:05.550
So, this is a small introduction.

00:22:05.551 --> 00:22:09.380
Let's now go back to
the second part of my talk.

00:22:09.381 --> 00:22:14.770
&gt;&gt; Why are you doing [inaudible].

00:22:14.771 --> 00:22:19.170
Why are you doing
such a complicated degeneration?

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:22.085
We really a benefit to that

00:22:22.086 --> 00:22:25.920
over generator in
field element [inaudible].

00:22:25.921 --> 00:22:30.080
&gt;&gt; The question is

00:22:30.081 --> 00:22:35.260
why I generate a random
bits in this fashion.

00:22:35.261 --> 00:22:38.885
Do you mean that
your question why

00:22:38.886 --> 00:22:40.700
the random bit function is

00:22:40.701 --> 00:22:42.800
implemented in the above fashion?

00:22:42.801 --> 00:22:44.170
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:48.270
&gt;&gt; Yes, that's thing, that's why,

00:22:48.271 --> 00:22:51.240
because I think there are
several random bit protocols

00:22:51.241 --> 00:22:56.980
and each of them have
some constraint.

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:01.380
One question is whether you want

00:23:01.381 --> 00:23:03.110
to have perfect correcting

00:23:03.111 --> 00:23:08.410
something and let's
say it's fine.

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:14.790
Okay, the next part
of my talk will

00:24:14.791 --> 00:24:20.025
be about secure linear algebra
over the rationals.

00:24:20.026 --> 00:24:23.985
This is a joint work
with Frank Blom,

00:24:23.986 --> 00:24:28.830
Berry Schoenmakers,
and Niels de Vreede.

00:24:28.831 --> 00:24:30.660
I want to rescope the setting,

00:24:30.661 --> 00:24:32.380
I'm still in secret sharing

00:24:32.381 --> 00:24:34.570
based multi-party
computation and I

00:24:34.571 --> 00:24:36.740
focus on the multi-party scenario

00:24:36.741 --> 00:24:39.600
with a larger or equal
than three players.

00:24:39.601 --> 00:24:43.500
I assume that I have

00:24:43.501 --> 00:24:48.550
some arithmetic black
box at my disposal,

00:24:48.551 --> 00:24:51.865
which provides me with
say an addition operation

00:24:51.866 --> 00:24:57.720
and multiplication operation
and opening values, etc.

00:24:57.721 --> 00:25:02.490
So, I don't specify exactly
which MPC framework I use.

00:25:02.491 --> 00:25:06.865
I view it as an API that
is there that I can use.

00:25:06.866 --> 00:25:11.480
Now my problem, that I want
to consider is that I have

00:25:11.481 --> 00:25:13.860
a full rank square matrix A

00:25:13.861 --> 00:25:17.460
and vector B with
integral coefficients,

00:25:17.461 --> 00:25:20.320
all these those entries

00:25:20.321 --> 00:25:23.720
are secret shared
among the parties.

00:25:24.230 --> 00:25:29.190
Now, I want to solve the
linear system AX equals B.

00:25:29.191 --> 00:25:31.570
So, I want to solve for x,

00:25:31.571 --> 00:25:35.400
I want to do this over
the rational numbers.

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:45.100
To use correct pronunciation.

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:50.935
So, what is the motivation here,

00:25:50.936 --> 00:25:53.860
I viewed irrational numbers as

00:25:53.861 --> 00:25:56.965
some kind of approximation
to the real numbers and

00:25:56.966 --> 00:26:01.870
engineers usually want to
solve linear algebra problems

00:26:01.871 --> 00:26:04.510
over the real numbers

00:26:04.511 --> 00:26:07.680
and if they come to
the cryptographers and they say,

00:26:07.681 --> 00:26:10.020
"Hey, we have
some privacy problem.

00:26:10.021 --> 00:26:11.395
We want to solve

00:26:11.396 --> 00:26:15.995
a linear algebra problem
in the context of MPC."

00:26:15.996 --> 00:26:18.720
Well, then maybe
the rational numbers are

00:26:18.721 --> 00:26:23.160
the closest arithmetic
structure to work over,

00:26:23.161 --> 00:26:25.785
which mimics the reals.

00:26:25.786 --> 00:26:28.290
We are interested in

00:26:28.291 --> 00:26:34.395
data processing applications
or secure statistics, etc.

00:26:34.396 --> 00:26:37.520
If we look at the related work,

00:26:37.521 --> 00:26:41.760
then there is related work
by Thomas Tuft from 2009,

00:26:41.761 --> 00:26:43.600
works over rationals in

00:26:43.601 --> 00:26:48.260
MPC in the context of
solving linear programs.

00:26:48.261 --> 00:26:49.830
Then the two party case,

00:26:49.831 --> 00:26:53.125
there are some references
mentioned there.

00:26:53.126 --> 00:26:58.970
But I focus on
the multi-party case.

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:03.465
If we think about performing

00:27:03.466 --> 00:27:06.775
arithmetic in
a finite field in MPC.

00:27:06.776 --> 00:27:09.330
Then think about
integer arithmetic

00:27:09.331 --> 00:27:11.530
versus rational arithmetic.

00:27:11.531 --> 00:27:14.469
Well, let's first say
that integer arithmetic

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:19.420
is easy to do.

00:27:19.421 --> 00:27:26.060
So, we have the correspondence
between the integers minus

00:27:26.061 --> 00:27:33.070
P over two to plus P over
two truncated or float,

00:27:33.071 --> 00:27:35.960
as long as we don't wrap
around the modulus,

00:27:35.961 --> 00:27:37.340
we keep this correspondence,

00:27:37.341 --> 00:27:38.690
so we can perform additions,

00:27:38.691 --> 00:27:40.860
multiplications, and it's as if,

00:27:40.861 --> 00:27:42.845
we were working over the integers

00:27:42.846 --> 00:27:44.770
when our prime is big enough.

00:27:44.771 --> 00:27:48.090
With rational arithmetic, we

00:27:48.091 --> 00:27:51.385
could do a division because
we're in a finite field.

00:27:51.386 --> 00:27:53.870
Then the division would be

00:27:53.871 --> 00:27:58.310
multiplication by
the multiplicative inverse of B.

00:27:58.311 --> 00:28:01.765
As long as A and B
are small enough,

00:28:01.766 --> 00:28:05.390
then there's still
a correspondence.

00:28:05.391 --> 00:28:08.760
It's just that if we would
look at this field element x.

00:28:08.761 --> 00:28:12.480
We don't recognize there
anything human, right?

00:28:12.481 --> 00:28:14.335
If, you would divide,

00:28:14.336 --> 00:28:16.510
if you would compute
two over three.

00:28:16.511 --> 00:28:18.905
You get some humongous
fields element

00:28:18.906 --> 00:28:21.810
and with rational reconstruction,

00:28:21.811 --> 00:28:25.215
you can still reconstruct

00:28:25.216 --> 00:28:29.295
these numerator and denominator,

00:28:29.296 --> 00:28:33.800
namely by performing
a lattice basis reduction.

00:28:33.801 --> 00:28:39.910
You encode the basis as
shown here on the slides.

00:28:39.911 --> 00:28:42.130
If you run Lagrange-Gauss,

00:28:42.131 --> 00:28:45.305
you will eventually,
get back A and B.

00:28:45.306 --> 00:28:49.010
The problem however, is
if you want to perform

00:28:49.011 --> 00:28:52.835
this in a secure
multi-party computation,

00:28:52.836 --> 00:28:58.050
you must be constant time
or data independent.

00:28:58.051 --> 00:29:00.660
So, you would have to
run this algorithm for

00:29:00.661 --> 00:29:03.330
the worst case and
pad it with with

00:29:03.331 --> 00:29:07.485
dummy operations.The
worst case was analyzed

00:29:07.486 --> 00:29:12.450
by Brigitte Vallee is
order log p iterations,

00:29:12.451 --> 00:29:13.870
where in every iteration,

00:29:13.871 --> 00:29:16.710
you would have to do
an integer divisions,

00:29:16.711 --> 00:29:18.459
so division with the truncation.

00:29:18.460 --> 00:29:22.815
So, that would be impractical
in multi-party computation.

00:29:22.816 --> 00:29:27.500
So, our approach is different.

00:29:27.501 --> 00:29:32.065
If, we look at the problem
of solving linear system.

00:29:32.066 --> 00:29:34.410
Well, let's just invert a matrix

00:29:34.411 --> 00:29:36.880
because we have
a full rank matrix.

00:29:36.881 --> 00:29:40.380
Numerical people would
never compute an inverse,

00:29:40.381 --> 00:29:42.500
but here, we are doing
exact arithmetic.

00:29:42.501 --> 00:29:46.440
So, we don't have condition
numbers to worry about.

00:29:46.441 --> 00:29:48.905
You can think about
the inverse as

00:29:48.906 --> 00:29:52.795
the adjugate matrix of A
divided by its determinants.

00:29:52.796 --> 00:29:57.450
Where both of these objects like

00:29:57.451 --> 00:30:02.695
the adjugate and the determinants
are over the integers.

00:30:02.696 --> 00:30:06.730
So again, if we have
a prime that is big enough,

00:30:06.731 --> 00:30:09.710
we can embed them in
the finite field.

00:30:09.711 --> 00:30:14.170
So, this representation
of the solution,

00:30:14.171 --> 00:30:16.230
prevents the occurrence of

00:30:16.231 --> 00:30:19.170
rational entries and with that,

00:30:19.171 --> 00:30:22.720
we don't need to
irrational reconstruction.

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:29.905
So, here we work
over the field of

00:30:29.906 --> 00:30:33.880
p. We are going to
compute the inverse of

00:30:33.881 --> 00:30:40.420
A via modified protocol
from Cramer and Damgard,

00:30:40.421 --> 00:30:45.260
which is in turn based on
Bar-llan and Beaver from '89.

00:30:45.261 --> 00:30:49.440
In this Cramer and Damgard
protocol which was

00:30:49.441 --> 00:30:51.870
allegedly presented for computing

00:30:51.871 --> 00:30:53.590
the inverse over finite fields,

00:30:53.591 --> 00:30:55.670
there we keep at all places

00:30:55.671 --> 00:30:58.495
the adjugate and
determinant separate.

00:30:58.496 --> 00:31:01.300
P must be large enough so that we

00:31:01.301 --> 00:31:03.960
don't go get a rep rounds,

00:31:03.961 --> 00:31:06.240
and here that's easy to compute.

00:31:06.241 --> 00:31:12.700
It's essentially the Hadamard
bounds for that matrix A.

00:31:12.701 --> 00:31:15.485
Here, or I call the matrix M,

00:31:15.486 --> 00:31:17.745
where I used to matrix A before.

00:31:17.746 --> 00:31:24.575
Anyway, this B is a bound on
the elements of that matrix,

00:31:24.576 --> 00:31:27.894
and then that's
the Hadamard inequality,

00:31:27.895 --> 00:31:30.930
where it essentially
means here is

00:31:30.931 --> 00:31:35.235
that I still have to
compute this product.

00:31:35.236 --> 00:31:40.394
So then in order to
represent that product,

00:31:40.395 --> 00:31:44.335
I need to slightly
increase P, of course.

00:31:44.336 --> 00:31:46.885
Well, the protocol
goes as follows.

00:31:46.886 --> 00:31:51.180
&gt;&gt; Does that mean that
the determinant should be huge?

00:31:51.181 --> 00:31:55.025
&gt;&gt; Yes, the determinant.
There's a question here that

00:31:55.026 --> 00:31:59.175
asks if the determinant
can get large or huge.

00:31:59.176 --> 00:32:00.580
Yes, that is indeed the case.

00:32:00.581 --> 00:32:04.795
So, we are doing
exact arithmetic here,

00:32:04.796 --> 00:32:10.390
which means that you have
an inherent size growth.

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:13.450
On the other hand,
if you would like to

00:32:13.451 --> 00:32:18.380
do approximative arithmetic or

00:32:18.381 --> 00:32:20.000
say floating point arithmetic,

00:32:20.001 --> 00:32:21.730
you would have to first built

00:32:21.731 --> 00:32:26.265
a floating-point computation
in multiparty computation,

00:32:26.266 --> 00:32:28.310
which is also expensive.

00:32:28.311 --> 00:32:30.710
So there's a trade off

00:32:30.711 --> 00:32:33.655
there and depending
on your application,

00:32:33.656 --> 00:32:36.665
you might use this approach.

00:32:36.666 --> 00:32:39.960
This works well for
small dimensions,

00:32:39.961 --> 00:32:41.480
and if you have, say,

00:32:41.481 --> 00:32:45.785
a prime of 200 bits
or thousand bits,

00:32:45.786 --> 00:32:51.980
you can still handle many cases.

00:32:51.981 --> 00:32:53.940
But on the other hand, if
you have a huge matrix,

00:32:53.941 --> 00:32:57.510
there's no way that
you are, well,

00:32:57.511 --> 00:33:00.470
this will not be
efficient because you

00:33:00.471 --> 00:33:03.310
have this big growth and you need

00:33:03.311 --> 00:33:05.730
a huge prime and making

00:33:05.731 --> 00:33:09.695
computations with
that big prime is heavy.

00:33:09.696 --> 00:33:15.090
So, indeed, there's
no free lunch.

00:33:17.180 --> 00:33:21.309
So the protocol goes as follows;

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:24.500
I know this matrix A has

00:33:24.501 --> 00:33:27.885
entries that are secret
shared over the field of

00:33:27.886 --> 00:33:29.860
p and we are going to sample

00:33:29.861 --> 00:33:34.740
a lower and upper triangular
matrix at random,

00:33:34.741 --> 00:33:37.715
where this L is actually
unite triangular.

00:33:37.716 --> 00:33:41.510
So it says has ones
on its diagonal.

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:46.320
Then we're going to compute
the product of this L and

00:33:46.321 --> 00:33:49.915
U which we call R. Let d be

00:33:49.916 --> 00:33:53.330
the determinant of
R. We can compute

00:33:53.331 --> 00:33:55.670
that determinant immediately from

00:33:55.671 --> 00:33:57.320
the diagonal elements of U.

00:33:57.321 --> 00:34:00.050
Now, we are going to

00:34:00.051 --> 00:34:04.720
compute the product of R and
A and reveal this product.

00:34:04.721 --> 00:34:07.500
So, we make the product public

00:34:07.501 --> 00:34:11.840
and it's this art and access
of blinding matrix for A.

00:34:11.841 --> 00:34:13.305
Now in the clear,

00:34:13.306 --> 00:34:17.080
we can compute the adjugate
and the determinant of A,

00:34:17.081 --> 00:34:19.750
for example with the
Bareiss algorithm.

00:34:19.751 --> 00:34:28.980
Then, well, we move to
blinding and and go back to

00:34:28.981 --> 00:34:31.730
the secret shared world by

00:34:31.731 --> 00:34:37.580
multiplying from the right
R and the inverse of

00:34:37.581 --> 00:34:41.125
the determinant to
get the adjugate of A

00:34:41.126 --> 00:34:44.730
and the determinant
of A is equal to

00:34:44.731 --> 00:34:47.420
the determinant of
RA times the inverse of

00:34:47.421 --> 00:34:55.010
d. If you would

00:34:55.011 --> 00:34:57.640
compare this algorithm to

00:34:57.641 --> 00:35:01.620
or this protocol to the one
from Cramer and Damgard,

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:08.110
making this L unit
triangular actually somewhat

00:35:08.111 --> 00:35:10.910
simplifies the proof
because in their protocol

00:35:10.911 --> 00:35:14.240
they do not use
a unit triangular matrix,

00:35:14.241 --> 00:35:16.310
and of course they don't keep

00:35:16.311 --> 00:35:19.320
this determinant and
adjugate separate.

00:35:20.510 --> 00:35:25.230
Yeah, this gives you
way a constant rounds

00:35:25.231 --> 00:35:32.070
or constants rounds
protocol with squared,

00:35:32.071 --> 00:35:34.780
order and squared local work.

00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:39.560
Here this n squared
assumes that I have

00:35:39.561 --> 00:35:44.295
a cheap inner product as Shamir
has cheap inner products.

00:35:44.296 --> 00:35:45.920
So that if I

00:35:45.921 --> 00:35:51.605
do vectors of length n and I
compute the inner product,

00:35:51.606 --> 00:35:55.830
that's as expensive as just
computing one multiplication.

00:35:55.831 --> 00:35:57.970
Because I can compute

00:35:57.971 --> 00:35:59.260
all these multiplications in

00:35:59.261 --> 00:36:00.935
parallel which is local work,

00:36:00.936 --> 00:36:04.920
and I don't count it and I
just have one re-sharing,

00:36:04.921 --> 00:36:09.470
so one interaction with
the other players.

00:36:11.980 --> 00:36:19.380
All right, now I want
to show this protocol,

00:36:19.381 --> 00:36:22.410
how you can implement
this in MPyC.

00:36:22.411 --> 00:36:25.020
So, I'm going to switch back to

00:36:25.021 --> 00:36:29.830
my mirror screen to the browser.

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:48.210
It could be bigger here.

00:36:48.211 --> 00:36:50.330
I'm not going line by

00:36:50.331 --> 00:36:52.260
line through this
implementation but I

00:36:52.261 --> 00:36:53.645
just want to see that

00:36:53.646 --> 00:36:55.860
if you are working
on such protocols,

00:36:55.861 --> 00:36:59.040
it's quite easy to
implement it in this MPyC

00:36:59.041 --> 00:37:03.410
and check whether you
didn't forget something.

00:37:03.411 --> 00:37:07.380
So, here is just an
implementation in

00:37:07.381 --> 00:37:10.220
plain Python of the
Bareiss algorithm

00:37:10.221 --> 00:37:11.500
and this computation is

00:37:11.501 --> 00:37:15.290
one that happens on
public data because it run on

00:37:15.291 --> 00:37:24.650
this non-secure part
or the opened matrix.

00:37:24.651 --> 00:37:26.780
So, well, we believe that that

00:37:26.781 --> 00:37:29.690
is a correct
implementation of Bareiss.

00:37:29.691 --> 00:37:32.985
Here, I wrote a wrapper

00:37:32.986 --> 00:37:37.235
to be able to use
some NumPy functions.

00:37:37.236 --> 00:37:40.120
For example, for the part where

00:37:40.121 --> 00:37:42.550
we have to sample
the random matrix,

00:37:42.551 --> 00:37:43.750
we are first creating

00:37:43.751 --> 00:37:47.590
this lower and upper
triangular matrix and NumPy

00:37:47.591 --> 00:37:50.170
provides these handy functions

00:37:50.171 --> 00:37:53.930
for arranging data at
a certain way in a matrix.

00:37:53.931 --> 00:37:56.060
I can reuse that actually for

00:37:56.061 --> 00:38:00.135
my secret matrices because NumPy,

00:38:00.136 --> 00:38:03.110
well, has a parameter
where you can specify what

00:38:03.111 --> 00:38:06.900
the element type of the matrices
or objects should be.

00:38:06.901 --> 00:38:10.680
So, this is another
example of where

00:38:10.681 --> 00:38:14.780
an MPC framework that
is implemented as

00:38:14.781 --> 00:38:19.230
a library has a flexible way

00:38:19.231 --> 00:38:20.890
of interacting with
the features of

00:38:20.891 --> 00:38:23.685
the host language which
I make use of here.

00:38:23.686 --> 00:38:29.960
So here, well, I create
a random vector.

00:38:30.390 --> 00:38:36.775
I divide or compute
the inverse of

00:38:36.776 --> 00:38:38.990
the product of
the diagonal elements of

00:38:38.991 --> 00:38:43.290
the matrix U and here
it is mpc.prods.

00:38:43.291 --> 00:38:46.075
That's a product tree,

00:38:46.076 --> 00:38:47.955
so it's a logarithmic depth,

00:38:47.956 --> 00:38:50.800
a product over a list.

00:38:50.801 --> 00:38:56.090
So it's a fault over list
that is running parallel.

00:39:00.330 --> 00:39:02.470
Here, I actually use

00:39:02.471 --> 00:39:06.980
the multiplication operator from

00:39:06.981 --> 00:39:11.715
NumPy and I apply that
to the secret matrices.

00:39:11.716 --> 00:39:13.530
But actually, MPyC there is now

00:39:13.531 --> 00:39:16.735
also a matrix product operator.

00:39:16.736 --> 00:39:19.935
Anyway, this function gives me

00:39:19.936 --> 00:39:22.950
the blinding matrix R and

00:39:22.951 --> 00:39:26.240
this inverted determinant
of the matrix.

00:39:26.241 --> 00:39:29.120
Then here, this is
the actual function

00:39:29.121 --> 00:39:32.000
which is a co-routine and

00:39:32.001 --> 00:39:35.190
MPyC makes use of asyncio which

00:39:35.191 --> 00:39:39.580
is the asynchronous
API of Python.

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:44.030
I think you have
function definitions

00:39:44.031 --> 00:39:48.440
that use the keyword
async, async def.

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:51.880
There's also
a decorator here which

00:39:51.881 --> 00:39:56.770
is from MPyC that does
some bookkeeping.

00:39:59.240 --> 00:40:02.880
Anyway, here's
the top-level script.

00:40:02.881 --> 00:40:05.590
I have a matrix,

00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:11.070
I secret share the elements and

00:40:11.071 --> 00:40:16.890
then I run the protocol
and I open the result.

00:40:16.891 --> 00:40:20.770
Here, well, we get the output.

00:40:20.771 --> 00:40:23.085
Again, this is just
to demonstrate

00:40:23.086 --> 00:40:25.570
that in a few lines of code,

00:40:25.571 --> 00:40:28.150
you can quickly
implement the protocol

00:40:28.151 --> 00:40:31.710
and see whether things work out.

00:40:31.711 --> 00:40:34.020
Questions about this part?

00:40:34.021 --> 00:40:37.890
&gt;&gt; Does MPyC look at
A and decide how big

00:40:37.891 --> 00:40:46.110
the modulus needs to
be and [inaudible].

00:40:46.111 --> 00:40:47.540
&gt;&gt; The question is,

00:40:47.541 --> 00:40:49.960
how does MPyC know the modulus?

00:40:49.961 --> 00:40:51.880
Well, there's
a default modulus that had

00:40:51.881 --> 00:40:54.280
picks if you don't
specify one and in

00:40:54.281 --> 00:40:55.360
this case we just get

00:40:55.361 --> 00:40:58.245
the default choice
because it allows me to

00:40:58.246 --> 00:41:03.135
compute up to 32 bits or
even bigger actually with,

00:41:03.136 --> 00:41:05.985
only the comparisons
are limited to this.

00:41:05.986 --> 00:41:07.660
But if I need a bigger modulus,

00:41:07.661 --> 00:41:09.935
I can just specify that.

00:41:09.936 --> 00:41:13.460
So, if you would have
an actual application,

00:41:13.461 --> 00:41:15.720
you would look at

00:41:15.721 --> 00:41:18.205
or find an upper bound
for the elements of

00:41:18.206 --> 00:41:19.860
A and then apply

00:41:19.861 --> 00:41:21.540
Hadamard algorithm and then or

00:41:21.541 --> 00:41:23.590
the Hadamard bounds
and based on that,

00:41:23.591 --> 00:41:24.880
it sets the correct

00:41:24.881 --> 00:41:27.175
prime or set the bit
length of the prime.

00:41:27.176 --> 00:41:28.460
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:41:34.400 --> 00:41:38.655
&gt;&gt; Okay, we have 15 minutes left.

00:41:38.656 --> 00:41:44.890
So, then I want to
continue our comparisons.

00:42:13.620 --> 00:42:19.300
So, in the '80s there

00:42:19.301 --> 00:42:25.655
was Andrew Yu who proposed
the millionaires problem,

00:42:25.656 --> 00:42:29.595
and today you would call it
the billionaires problem,

00:42:29.596 --> 00:42:33.240
and they want to know who is the

00:42:33.241 --> 00:42:38.220
richest without revealing
their account statements.

00:42:38.221 --> 00:42:41.160
But let's assume that
these billionaires are

00:42:41.161 --> 00:42:43.720
extremely paranoid and they don't

00:42:43.721 --> 00:42:46.964
want to run a
two-party MPC protocol

00:42:46.965 --> 00:42:49.000
because they are
afraid that somebody

00:42:49.001 --> 00:42:54.915
has a quantum computer in
his backyard and text protocol.

00:42:54.916 --> 00:43:00.550
So, let's go to a three
party protocol where MPC can

00:43:00.551 --> 00:43:07.540
actually offer a quantum
resistant solution.

00:43:07.890 --> 00:43:11.610
Yu, who's probably
not a billionaire,

00:43:11.611 --> 00:43:15.055
maybe he'a a millionaire
I'm not sure,

00:43:15.056 --> 00:43:19.760
but he just runs as
a third party as a helper party.

00:43:19.761 --> 00:43:21.700
So, he is involved in

00:43:21.701 --> 00:43:25.340
the protocol but he does
not provide any inputs,

00:43:25.341 --> 00:43:27.550
and he does not get
to see any output.

00:43:27.551 --> 00:43:31.170
But he's just there
as a helper party.

00:43:32.200 --> 00:43:36.630
Warren Buffet and Bill Gates,

00:43:36.631 --> 00:43:39.435
they essentially want
to do a comparison.

00:43:39.436 --> 00:43:43.055
So, you have two inputs a and b.

00:43:43.056 --> 00:43:44.720
You can subtract them,

00:43:44.721 --> 00:43:48.630
and then determine the
sign of the difference.

00:43:48.631 --> 00:43:49.895
That would be comparison.

00:43:49.896 --> 00:43:53.050
Then of course you can
distinguish between,

00:43:53.051 --> 00:43:56.060
say the proper signum function,

00:43:56.061 --> 00:43:59.605
where you have also a
zero here as outputs.

00:43:59.606 --> 00:44:02.620
This is a ternary output
or you have the greater

00:44:02.621 --> 00:44:06.595
or equal than zero function
which has a binary output.

00:44:06.596 --> 00:44:08.725
In this part of the talk,

00:44:08.726 --> 00:44:15.430
I want to evaluate the sign
using Quadratic Reciduosity.

00:44:15.500 --> 00:44:20.350
So, if you carefully
pick a prime,

00:44:20.351 --> 00:44:23.030
then you could have
the situation where

00:44:23.031 --> 00:44:27.960
the Legendre symbol coincides
with the sine of a number,

00:44:27.961 --> 00:44:31.825
over certain range around zero.

00:44:31.826 --> 00:44:39.905
Where this range -d up to
+d can be of size order L,

00:44:39.906 --> 00:44:42.770
where L is the bit
length of the prime.

00:44:42.771 --> 00:44:49.060
This idea goes back
to Feigh from 94 that

00:44:49.061 --> 00:44:52.010
used this trick to
compute the sign of

00:44:52.011 --> 00:44:56.785
a number in an interval
minus two to plus two in F7,

00:44:56.786 --> 00:44:59.835
and more recently by you,

00:44:59.836 --> 00:45:04.110
and this was explored for
much bigger primes and

00:45:04.111 --> 00:45:08.280
see how you can build
a comparison protocol from that.

00:45:08.281 --> 00:45:12.845
That's on eprint,
the paper by you.

00:45:12.846 --> 00:45:17.410
For example, for the prime 311,

00:45:17.411 --> 00:45:19.930
which is by the way Blum prime.

00:45:19.931 --> 00:45:22.315
Because it's a Blum prime,

00:45:22.316 --> 00:45:25.580
minus one is not a square.

00:45:25.581 --> 00:45:28.380
So, we have nice
odd symmetry around

00:45:28.381 --> 00:45:33.335
zero in the Legendre symbols.

00:45:33.336 --> 00:45:38.125
Here up to 10 or up to 11,

00:45:38.126 --> 00:45:41.860
and including 10, we have
this correspondence between

00:45:41.861 --> 00:45:45.495
the Legendre symbol and
the sign of the numbers.

00:45:45.496 --> 00:45:49.270
Because of this Blum property,

00:45:49.271 --> 00:45:55.520
things look the same or
mirrored at the negative part.

00:45:57.560 --> 00:46:01.510
Yes. So, this could
be viewed as a way

00:46:01.511 --> 00:46:05.870
of determining the sign of
numbers in a small range.

00:46:06.350 --> 00:46:13.325
We can use this idea also
in an MPC I should say,

00:46:13.326 --> 00:46:16.060
if we can efficiently

00:46:16.061 --> 00:46:19.750
evaluate securely
the Legendre symbol.

00:46:20.560 --> 00:46:23.130
The complete multiplicativity of

00:46:23.131 --> 00:46:25.060
Legendre symbol admits the

00:46:25.061 --> 00:46:29.330
following for clover protocol
for computing it.

00:46:29.331 --> 00:46:31.915
So first, in an offline phase,

00:46:31.916 --> 00:46:34.270
we would compute
a random field elements

00:46:34.271 --> 00:46:36.850
together with its sign.

00:46:36.851 --> 00:46:39.045
Then in the online face,

00:46:39.046 --> 00:46:41.580
the x here is the input,

00:46:41.581 --> 00:46:45.185
we multiply it by
this random field elements.

00:46:45.186 --> 00:46:48.744
So, we blind it, then we
can open this product,

00:46:48.745 --> 00:46:54.610
and we then can compute the
Legendre symbol of that C as

00:46:54.611 --> 00:46:58.580
a clear text computation
and compute it with

00:46:58.581 --> 00:47:03.610
the secret shared sign of
that pair to get the results.

00:47:03.611 --> 00:47:05.795
Can do this in
one communication round.

00:47:05.796 --> 00:47:07.570
There's one caveat here,

00:47:07.571 --> 00:47:11.750
namely that we cannot use
this to evaluate zero,

00:47:11.751 --> 00:47:14.775
because then we would kill
here immediately the product,

00:47:14.776 --> 00:47:19.590
and the adversary can see
that we input zero there.

00:47:19.591 --> 00:47:23.070
But let's ignore
this for this talk.

00:47:23.071 --> 00:47:25.710
We have written a paper,

00:47:25.711 --> 00:47:27.540
and of course there
we do care about it,

00:47:27.541 --> 00:47:31.110
but here let's just assume
as if we can compute

00:47:31.111 --> 00:47:33.980
the Legendre symbol securely
and not worry about

00:47:33.981 --> 00:47:37.715
the zero that we actually
have to circumvent.

00:47:37.716 --> 00:47:41.580
&gt;&gt; In your case the time
did not have to be enormous

00:47:41.581 --> 00:47:44.700
to have this property that
you need or would expect,

00:47:44.701 --> 00:47:46.865
if you want the interval to be

00:47:46.866 --> 00:47:49.890
non-trivial the prime would
be ridiculously large.

00:47:49.891 --> 00:47:51.305
&gt;&gt; There's a question here

00:47:51.306 --> 00:47:53.350
whether the prime has
to be ridiculously

00:47:53.351 --> 00:47:58.490
large or to end up with
a usable or reasonable interval.

00:47:58.491 --> 00:48:03.310
Yes. There is indeed this log B.

00:48:03.311 --> 00:48:05.330
But I do have a table at the end

00:48:05.331 --> 00:48:07.880
of the talk that shows
some practical values,

00:48:07.881 --> 00:48:10.990
and indeed you won't

00:48:10.991 --> 00:48:14.950
have an extremely
large comparison range

00:48:14.951 --> 00:48:16.850
or sign evaluation range.

00:48:16.851 --> 00:48:18.950
But still for
certain applications

00:48:18.951 --> 00:48:20.960
it could be interesting.

00:48:20.961 --> 00:48:23.460
But let's come back
to that later.

00:48:23.650 --> 00:48:27.180
Anyway, this is basically

00:48:27.181 --> 00:48:31.680
the idea of Feige
and this Yu paper.

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:36.760
Now, the question is how
can we increase the range?

00:48:36.761 --> 00:48:40.195
Of course, we can just
pick a bigger prime,

00:48:40.196 --> 00:48:44.220
and then we will get
a somewhat bigger range.

00:48:44.221 --> 00:48:45.940
But the question that we

00:48:45.941 --> 00:48:47.540
posed in this work
is whether we can

00:48:47.541 --> 00:48:49.250
get a bigger range while we

00:48:49.251 --> 00:48:52.400
keep the bit length
of the prime fixed.

00:48:52.410 --> 00:48:55.300
Why would we be
interested in that?

00:48:55.301 --> 00:49:00.760
Well, there's a cost associated
to increasing a prime.

00:49:00.830 --> 00:49:04.060
Well, in this work what we do,

00:49:04.061 --> 00:49:07.795
if we look again
at this 311 prime,

00:49:07.796 --> 00:49:11.620
then if you look at
this error, so to speak,

00:49:11.621 --> 00:49:13.740
then the sign function

00:49:13.741 --> 00:49:16.910
is not such an interesting
function, right?

00:49:16.911 --> 00:49:18.655
There's not much happening here.

00:49:18.656 --> 00:49:20.715
It's just a constant line.

00:49:20.716 --> 00:49:23.910
Only some interesting stuff
happens around zero,

00:49:23.911 --> 00:49:25.165
where you have to transition.

00:49:25.166 --> 00:49:27.950
But here you could actually
peek at your neighbors,

00:49:27.951 --> 00:49:30.215
and do a majority vote,

00:49:30.216 --> 00:49:33.070
and repair that error.

00:49:33.071 --> 00:49:36.625
So, you take the sum

00:49:36.626 --> 00:49:40.315
of the Legendre symbols of
your neighbors and yourself,

00:49:40.316 --> 00:49:44.220
then you get in a range of
minus three plus three,

00:49:44.221 --> 00:49:46.590
and then you would
have to compute

00:49:46.591 --> 00:49:49.555
the sign of this y of x.

00:49:49.556 --> 00:49:53.250
You can do that with
yet another invocation

00:49:53.251 --> 00:49:56.025
of the Legendre protocol,

00:49:56.026 --> 00:50:01.010
or you could use a polynomial
to find that sign.

00:50:01.011 --> 00:50:08.400
Well, in this case we look at
one neighbor at each side.

00:50:08.401 --> 00:50:10.410
So, let's say K equals one,

00:50:10.411 --> 00:50:12.380
and we have a window length

00:50:12.381 --> 00:50:14.055
of length three in this example.

00:50:14.056 --> 00:50:16.065
So, 2k+1.

00:50:16.066 --> 00:50:18.720
But we can generalize
that and have

00:50:18.721 --> 00:50:20.770
the following definition where we

00:50:20.771 --> 00:50:24.200
define the k range of P.

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:27.355
Well, where you take a bigger

00:50:27.356 --> 00:50:29.820
'k' and hence a bigger window,

00:50:29.821 --> 00:50:34.380
and you call 'd' the
largest integer such

00:50:34.381 --> 00:50:40.160
that you still have
correctness for this method.

00:50:40.161 --> 00:50:43.810
We set d_k(p) to zero,

00:50:43.811 --> 00:50:48.820
if you cannot find such a 'd'.

00:50:48.821 --> 00:50:51.850
With this definition, we can

00:50:51.851 --> 00:50:55.855
now go to the table
of some examples.

00:50:55.856 --> 00:51:02.170
Here the first column
gives you the 'd_0(p)',

00:51:02.171 --> 00:51:08.680
which exceed
the Fieger Style Range,

00:51:08.681 --> 00:51:11.480
and if you have window length

00:51:11.481 --> 00:51:14.230
of three then you
get this 'd_1(p)',

00:51:14.231 --> 00:51:17.280
and if you have a
window length of five,

00:51:17.281 --> 00:51:19.330
so you look at two neighbors on

00:51:19.331 --> 00:51:21.975
the left on the right you
have got this 'd_2(p)',

00:51:21.976 --> 00:51:23.980
and if we look for example,

00:51:23.981 --> 00:51:26.335
here at the 32-bits prime,

00:51:26.336 --> 00:51:29.665
where we get the range of 96.

00:51:29.666 --> 00:51:33.760
Here if we look at 32-bit prime,

00:51:33.761 --> 00:51:37.990
we get threefold improvement
and here you have

00:51:37.991 --> 00:51:43.880
already eight bits
range plus sign,

00:51:43.881 --> 00:51:45.625
so, essentially nine bits.

00:51:45.626 --> 00:51:47.820
So, there could be applications

00:51:47.821 --> 00:51:49.490
where you are
interested in working

00:51:49.491 --> 00:51:54.545
with this low or
medium range numbers.

00:51:54.546 --> 00:51:58.910
If we go to k equals
two for a 32-bits,

00:51:58.911 --> 00:52:03.545
we would actually be here is 458.

00:52:03.546 --> 00:52:05.570
So, compared to the zero,

00:52:05.571 --> 00:52:09.845
that's almost a five fold
increase in the range.

00:52:09.846 --> 00:52:13.730
Well, I've-, question, sure.

00:52:17.370 --> 00:52:21.370
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] Is
there any trade-offs?

00:52:21.371 --> 00:52:24.670
I mean, can you compute
five [inaudible]

00:52:24.671 --> 00:52:28.545
cheaper than one other
than five single once?

00:52:28.546 --> 00:52:32.920
&gt;&gt; That's a question that
you'll also have to increase

00:52:32.921 --> 00:52:38.550
the local work, yes.

00:52:38.551 --> 00:52:40.810
That's true, that's a trade-off,

00:52:40.811 --> 00:52:44.445
you still need to compute
those resemblance symbols.

00:52:44.446 --> 00:52:47.020
I'm not aware of a method

00:52:47.021 --> 00:52:52.400
to compute them all together
in a more clever way,

00:52:53.740 --> 00:52:59.565
but indeed you do have to
do more local local work.

00:52:59.566 --> 00:53:04.445
But one example where it
could pay off is that,

00:53:04.446 --> 00:53:07.670
say you are on
the embedded platform

00:53:07.671 --> 00:53:10.760
and you also have
memory restriction,

00:53:10.761 --> 00:53:13.200
so you don't want your numbers to

00:53:13.201 --> 00:53:16.270
be too big because you
have to store them,

00:53:16.271 --> 00:53:22.100
then maybe you can exchange
space for time and yeah.

00:53:22.830 --> 00:53:27.620
&gt;&gt; Did you also look
at different primes,

00:53:27.621 --> 00:53:31.235
using C3 primes and then
taking the majority vote?

00:53:31.236 --> 00:53:33.610
&gt;&gt; Another question here
is whether we looked

00:53:33.611 --> 00:53:36.100
at taking different primes and

00:53:36.101 --> 00:53:38.510
then take a majority vote between

00:53:38.511 --> 00:53:41.500
the results within
those prime fields,

00:53:41.501 --> 00:53:45.070
and the answer is no, we did not.

00:53:45.071 --> 00:53:46.655
I think you can do that,

00:53:46.656 --> 00:53:49.285
would be an interesting
direction to look into.

00:53:49.286 --> 00:53:53.700
But also with, say
the setting in mind

00:53:53.701 --> 00:53:58.360
where we usually work
over single prime fields.

00:53:58.361 --> 00:53:59.710
&gt;&gt; They would have to be shared.

00:53:59.711 --> 00:54:01.255
&gt;&gt; Yes you would have to do

00:54:01.256 --> 00:54:04.690
things between
those prime fields.

00:54:04.691 --> 00:54:06.760
So, there would probably be

00:54:06.761 --> 00:54:10.410
modulus conversions be involved,

00:54:10.411 --> 00:54:12.510
which are not for free.

00:54:13.970 --> 00:54:19.485
So, we applied
these comparison methods

00:54:19.486 --> 00:54:21.390
to the evaluation of

00:54:21.391 --> 00:54:24.850
neural networks and this is

00:54:24.851 --> 00:54:28.565
a multilayer perceptron from

00:54:28.566 --> 00:54:33.365
a paper from 2016 from
Courbariaux et al,

00:54:33.366 --> 00:54:35.270
which is for eminists.

00:54:35.271 --> 00:54:38.140
So, here we have
this handwritten digit,

00:54:38.141 --> 00:54:41.415
and we want to
recognize the digit,

00:54:41.416 --> 00:54:44.085
and we want to do this or

00:54:44.086 --> 00:54:47.870
evaluate this in
multiparty computation.

00:54:47.871 --> 00:54:52.790
So, how the multilayer
perceptron works is,

00:54:52.791 --> 00:54:54.750
well it's a bit small here,

00:54:54.751 --> 00:54:58.425
but it's- there's a matrix of

00:54:58.426 --> 00:55:00.790
parameters and the elements of

00:55:00.791 --> 00:55:03.075
this matrix are minus
one or plus one,

00:55:03.076 --> 00:55:04.680
so binary in some sense,

00:55:04.681 --> 00:55:08.285
and you do a
matrix-vector product,

00:55:08.286 --> 00:55:10.440
where the vector at
zero is actually

00:55:10.441 --> 00:55:15.190
the image that has been
flattened to a vector.

00:55:15.670 --> 00:55:19.060
Then there's also bias factor
that you add there,

00:55:19.061 --> 00:55:22.320
and here the non-linearity
is taking the sign.

00:55:22.321 --> 00:55:24.830
So, that maps well
with our work here,

00:55:24.831 --> 00:55:26.110
and then you do

00:55:26.111 --> 00:55:29.010
these matrix vector products

00:55:29.011 --> 00:55:32.325
and taking science through
a couple of layers,

00:55:32.326 --> 00:55:36.840
and at some point you end up
with a vector of length 10,

00:55:36.841 --> 00:55:39.350
where each entry gives you

00:55:39.351 --> 00:55:41.670
an estimate of
the probability that's

00:55:41.671 --> 00:55:47.225
the digit is
that particular number.

00:55:47.226 --> 00:55:49.470
So, the probability
for that it's a zero,

00:55:49.471 --> 00:55:51.290
probability for one et cetera,

00:55:51.291 --> 00:55:54.885
and then you could
take an arc marks.

00:55:54.886 --> 00:56:01.780
Yeah, so we have implemented
this in M part C,

00:56:01.781 --> 00:56:06.325
and report on the results
in an upcoming paper.

00:56:06.326 --> 00:56:08.805
Okay, this concludes my talk.

00:56:08.806 --> 00:56:11.690
Thank you very much
and I think, yes.

00:56:11.691 --> 00:56:15.500
I stayed within
an hour, thank you.

00:56:17.370 --> 00:56:20.150
Any further questions
for the room?

00:56:20.151 --> 00:56:21.825
&gt;&gt; So, I have a question about

00:56:21.826 --> 00:56:24.940
the linear algebra evaluation.

00:56:24.941 --> 00:56:28.485
So, you sampled
some random matrices L and U,

00:56:28.486 --> 00:56:31.440
and then you publish
the L times U

00:56:31.441 --> 00:56:34.250
basically times
the secret shared measures.

00:56:34.251 --> 00:56:36.490
So, can you prove

00:56:36.491 --> 00:56:37.540
that doesn't leak any

00:56:37.541 --> 00:56:38.825
information about
the secret matrix?

00:56:38.826 --> 00:56:44.225
&gt;&gt; There's a question about
the linear algebra protocol,

00:56:44.226 --> 00:56:48.070
whether the blinding operation
and then opening

00:56:48.071 --> 00:56:52.765
the blinded matrix does not
leak sensitive information.

00:56:52.766 --> 00:56:55.485
Well, the answer is
yes. There is a proof,

00:56:55.486 --> 00:56:59.370
and the proof largely follows
the proof from [inaudible] ,

00:56:59.371 --> 00:57:02.740
but it even simplifies
a bit because we've got

00:57:02.741 --> 00:57:08.365
notice unit triangular
structure on L,

00:57:08.366 --> 00:57:12.160
and then it's as easy as
showing that there is

00:57:12.161 --> 00:57:17.750
a bijection so that
things are fine.

00:57:17.751 --> 00:57:19.960
&gt;&gt; Okay, and also.

00:57:19.961 --> 00:57:23.160
So, for the determinant
evaluation,

00:57:23.161 --> 00:57:25.245
so you sometimes
need to divide by

00:57:25.246 --> 00:57:27.895
the determinant which is,

00:57:27.896 --> 00:57:30.395
because of these samples,

00:57:30.396 --> 00:57:33.845
those random matrices kind
of uniformly randomly.

00:57:33.846 --> 00:57:35.500
The determinants can be like

00:57:35.501 --> 00:57:37.960
a random number in
the finite field right?

00:57:37.961 --> 00:57:41.790
So, it's not necessarily
smaller than route p. So,

00:57:41.791 --> 00:57:42.900
in that case can you still

00:57:42.901 --> 00:57:44.535
do the rational reconstruction,

00:57:44.536 --> 00:57:46.960
when the denominator
can be arbitrary?

00:57:46.961 --> 00:57:52.595
&gt;&gt; There's a question
about the determinants

00:57:52.596 --> 00:57:56.460
and dividing out
these determinants

00:57:56.461 --> 00:57:59.630
and also in relation to
rational reconstruction.

00:57:59.631 --> 00:58:01.730
First thing to mention is that,

00:58:01.731 --> 00:58:05.815
we do not use rational
reconstruction because we

00:58:05.816 --> 00:58:08.015
circumvent it by keeping

00:58:08.016 --> 00:58:10.210
the educator and
determinant separate.

00:58:10.211 --> 00:58:15.100
So, we never perform a division
that is known exact.

00:58:15.150 --> 00:58:18.435
Maybe that already
answers your question?

00:58:18.436 --> 00:58:18.905
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:58:18.906 --> 00:58:23.460
&gt;&gt; But otherwise let's
take take it offline.

00:58:26.910 --> 00:58:28.270
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] . Okay?

00:58:28.271 --> 00:58:30.050
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

